 "evidence": {
  "S1": {
   "id": "S1",
   "kind": "document",
   "title": "Statistics Exercise Guide",
   "text": "Problem 3: Confidence Interval for a Mean\nQuestion. A sample of n = 36 students has mean study time 12.4 hours per week. The population standard deviation is known to be 3 hours. Build a 95% confidence interval.\nSolution. The standard error is 3 / sqrt(36) = 0.5. The margin of error is 1.96 * 0.5 = 0.98. The interval is 12.4 plus or minus 0.98, that is (11.42, 13.38). Interpretation: the procedure captures the true mean in about 95% of repeated samples. If the sample size were four times larger (n = 144) the standard error would halve to 0.25 and the interval would become narrower.",
   "doc_id": "statistics_exercise_guide",
   "course_id": "statistics",
   "location": "Problem 3: Confidence Interval for a Mean",
   "chunk_id": "statistics_exercise_guide:0003",
   "score": 0.4998,
   "data": {
    "file_type": "docx",
    "page": null,
    "section": "Problem 3: Confidence Interval for a Mean",
    "filename": "statistics_exercise_guide.docx"
   },
   "derived_from": []
  },
  "S2": {
   "id": "S2",
   "kind": "document",
   "title": "Statistics Estimators And Bias",
   "text": "2. Variance of an Estimator\nThe variance of an estimator describes how much the estimate changes from one random sample\nto the next. For the sample mean it equals sigma squared divided by n, so the standard error of the\nmean is sigma divided by the square root of n.\nThis is not the same quantity as the variance of the data themselves, which describes the spread\nof individual observations around their mean. Quadrupling the sample size halves the standard\nerror but does not change the variance of the data.",
   "doc_id": "statistics_estimators_and_bias",
   "course_id": "statistics",
   "location": "p.2",
   "chunk_id": "statistics_estimators_and_bias:0001",
   "score": 0.4468,
   "data": {
    "file_type": "pdf",
    "page": 2,
    "section": "2. Variance of an Estimator",
    "filename": "statistics_estimators_and_bias.pdf"
   },
   "derived_from": []
  },
  "S3": {
   "id": "S3",
   "kind": "document",
   "title": "Statistics Reference Notes",
   "text": "DESCRIPTIVE FORMULAS\nMean: x-bar = (sum of x) / n\nPopulation variance: sigma^2 = sum((x - mu)^2) / N\nSample variance: s^2 = sum((x - x-bar)^2) / (n - 1)\nStandard deviation: the square root of the variance.\nIQR = Q3 - Q1. Outlier fences: Q1 - 1.5*IQR and Q3 + 1.5*IQR.\nCoefficient of variation: CV = standard deviation / mean.",
   "doc_id": "statistics_reference_notes",
   "course_id": "statistics",
   "location": "DESCRIPTIVE FORMULAS",
   "chunk_id": "statistics_reference_notes:0001",
   "score": 0.4821,
   "data": {
    "file_type": "txt",
    "page": null,
    "section": "DESCRIPTIVE FORMULAS",
    "filename": "statistics_reference_notes.txt"
   },
   "derived_from": []
  },
  "S4": {
   "id": "S4",
   "kind": "document",
   "title": "Statistics Lecture Notes",
   "text": "mu and standard deviation sigma. The empirical rule says about 68% of values lie within one standard deviation of the  mean, 95% within two and 99.7% within three. A z-score z = (x - mu) / sigma tells how many standard deviations a value is from the mean. The standard normal distribution has mean 0 and standard deviation 1.",
   "doc_id": "statistics_lecture_notes",
   "course_id": "statistics",
   "location": "p.4",
   "chunk_id": "statistics_lecture_notes:0006",
   "score": 0.4283,
   "data": {
