  "S6": {
   "id": "S6",
   "kind": "document",
   "title": "Statistics Exercise Guide",
   "text": "Problem 4: One-Sample Hypothesis Test\nQuestion. A factory claims bulbs last 1000 hours on average. A sample of 25 bulbs has mean 980 and sample standard deviation 50. Test H0: mu = 1000 against H1: mu is not 1000 at alpha = 0.05.\nSolution. The t statistic is (980 - 1000) / (50 / sqrt(25)) = -20 / 10 = -2.0 with 24 degrees of freedom. The two-sided critical value is about 2.064. Because the absolute value 2.0 is smaller than 2.064 we fail to reject H0 at the 5% level. Failing to reject does not prove the claim is true; the sample may simply lack the power to detect a 20 hour difference.",
   "doc_id": "statistics_exercise_guide",
   "course_id": "statistics",
   "location": "Problem 4: One-Sample Hypothesis Test",
