  "S8": {
   "id": "S8",
   "kind": "document",
   "title": "Statistics Lecture Notes",
   "text": "size grows. A confidence interval gives a range of plausible values for a parameter. A 95% confidence interval for  a mean with known sigma is x-bar plus or minus 1.96 * sigma / sqrt(n). When sigma is unknown and n is small, replace 1.96 with a critical value from the t distribution with n - 1 degrees of freedom. A 95% confidence level means that if the procedure were repeated many times, about 95% of the intervals would contain the true parameter; it does not mean there is a 95% probability that one specific computed interval contains it.",
